=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The campus DNS resolver on srv1 (10.5.0.4, running ISC BIND 9 / named) has been
misconfigured with "recursion no" in /etc/bind/named.conf. BIND is only
authoritative for the internal zone "jeremysitlab.com" and refuses all recursive
resolution requests for external domain names. Every DNS query for an external
hostname (e.g. google.com) receives a REFUSED response with the flag "recursion
not available," so PC1's browser can never resolve public URLs — pages spin and
time out, exactly as reported.

IP-layer connectivity is fully intact: PC1 can ping its gateway (10.1.0.1),
ISPA's edge interface (203.0.113.1), and the simulated internet loopback
(172.253.62.100) with zero loss. The fault is purely at the application-layer
name-resolution stage. OSPF is converged, the default route is distributed by r1
via "default-information originate," and ISPA correctly routes return traffic back
to the campus — none of those components is the problem.

The root cause is a single option in /etc/bind/named.conf on srv1: "recursion no;"
was set (with an inline comment noting "offline lab, no upstream") and the forwarders
block in named.conf.options was left commented out, leaving no mechanism to resolve
external hostnames.

Corrective fix: On srv1, edit /etc/bind/named.conf and change "recursion no" to
"recursion yes". Add a "forwarders { <upstream-IP>; };" stanza pointing to a
reachable upstream resolver (e.g. one of the ISPA/ISPB loopbacks if they run a
resolver, or a real public resolver if the lab has external internet access), then
reload BIND with "rndc reload" or "systemctl reload named."
=== END ===
```